<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Tatar confederation</h1><p class="st-meta">900 CE – 1202 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A powerful group of tribes in eastern Mongolia, enemies of Genghis Khan's family, whose name Europeans later applied to all the Mongol peoples.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> appear in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-turkic">Old Turkic</a> inscriptions of the 8th century as the Otuz Tatar and Toquz Tatar. By the 10th century tribes under this name occupied the grasslands of eastern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mongolia">Mongolia</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulun-lake">Lakes Hulun</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-buir">Buir</a>, a numerous and wealthy people organised in several clans under their own chiefs.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>They lived by pastoral nomadism, herding horses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and sheep, and traded and fought with their settled neighbours. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty</a> (1115-1234) used them as allies against other steppe peoples; according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-history-of-the-mongols">Secret History of the Mongols</a> they handed the Mongol leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambaghai">Ambaghai</a> to the Jin for execution and poisoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Temüjin's</a> father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yesugei">Yesügei</a>. There was no lasting central government.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1196 the Jin, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Temüjin</a> and the Keraite ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ong-khan">Toghrul</a>, defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meguejin-seultue">Megüjin Seultü</a>. In 1202 Temüjin crushed the remaining Tatars and had most of their men killed, absorbing the survivors into the future <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tatar-confederation.jpg" alt="Tatar confederation" width="320" height="209"><figcaption>Image: Khiruge · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mongol_Empire_c.1207.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 CE – 1202 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Megüjin Seultü</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tengrism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mongolic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, <a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tatars (Mongolian Plateau)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1051&amp;focus=tatar-confederation" aria-label="Tatar confederation on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7131 556 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tatar confederation lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8012 829h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7451 812h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7451 812h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Mongolia. <a href="/atlas?year=1051&amp;focus=tatar-confederation">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

